STEP 1 — CREATE AZURE RESOURCES

Set your variables:

resourceGroup=myResourceGrouplod60458567
location=eastus
keyVaultName=mykeyvaultname60458567

Create the Key Vault:

az keyvault create --name $keyVaultName \
    --resource-group $resourceGroup \
    --location $location

The lab also notes that in your Cloudslice setup, the resource group may already exist, so that step can be skipped if it’s already provisioned.

STEP 2 — ASSIGN YOURSELF PERMISSIONS

Get your signed-in user principal name:

userPrincipal=$(az rest --method GET --url https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me \
    --headers 'Content-Type=application/json' \
    --query userPrincipalName --output tsv)

Get the Key Vault resource ID:

resourceID=$(az keyvault show --resource-group $resourceGroup \
    --name $keyVaultName --query id --output tsv)

Assign the Key Vault Secrets Officer role:

az role assignment create --assignee $userPrincipal \
    --role "Key Vault Secrets Officer" \
    --scope $resourceID

This role is what allows your account to set, delete, and list secrets in the vault.

STEP 3 — ADD A SECRET USING AZURE CLI

Create a sample secret:

az keyvault secret set --vault-name $keyVaultName \
    --name "MySecret" \
    --value "My secret value"

Verify it:

az keyvault secret show --name "MySecret" --vault-name $keyVaultName

The lab explicitly uses this as a quick validation before moving to the .NET app.

